==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷem-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|convenir}}, from {{der|en|la|convenio|convenio, convenire|come together}}, from {{affix|la|con-|gloss1=with, together|veniō|gloss2=come|nocat=1}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷm̥yéti}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*gʷem-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈviːn/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-convene.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈvin/|a=US,CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈviːn/|[kənˈvɪin]|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come together; to [[meet]]; to [[unite]].
#* {{RQ:Newton Opticks|passage=In short-sighted men {{...}} the rays converge and '''convene''' in the eyes before they come at the bottom.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come together, as in one body or for a public purpose; to meet; to [[assemble]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1670|author={{w|Richard Baker (chronicler)|Richard Baker}}|title=A Chronicle of the Kings of England from the Time of the Romans Government unto the Death of King James
|passage=The Parliament of Scotland now '''convened'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Thomson Summer|year=1768|passage=Faint, underneath, the household fowls '''convene'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|to cause to assemble, to call together}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to assemble; to call together; to [[convoke]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[summon]] [[judicially]] to meet or appear.
# {{lb|en|transitive|with "on" or "upon"}} To make a [[convention]]; to [[declare]] a [[rule]] by convention.
#: {{ux|en|To forestall any problems, we '''convened on''' the rule that all the database records would avoid containing certain literal strings.}}

